[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
## TL;DR  

**MapLibre Native on Android can render all labels from fonts that live on the device – you just have to:**

1. **Bundle the font files (TTF/OTF) in your APK** (e.g. `src/main/assets/fonts/…`).  
2. **Add a `font-faces` block to your style** that points to those files with a URL that the native SDK can resolve (`asset://…` or `file:///android_asset/…`).  
3. **Give each `text-font` name in the style a matching entry in `font-faces`.**  
4. **Provide a *dummy* `glyphs` URL** (the native SDK still expects the field, but it will never be used for the scripts you care about).  
5. **Load the style locally** (or from the network) and call `style.setFontFaces()` (or embed the block in the JSON) before the map draws.

That’s it – no external glyph-PBF server, no network, and all scripts (Latin, CJK, Arabic, Devanagari, etc.) are drawn with the on-device fonts.

Below is a step-by-step guide, a minimal working example, and a checklist of gotchas.

---

## 1. Why a “dummy” `glyphs` field is still required

*MapLibre Native* (as of v11.13.0) **requires the `glyphs` property to exist** in the style JSON.  
If it is missing the map will fail to initialise (open issue [#165](https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-native/issues/165)).  

The SDK **does not actually request the PBF files** when a matching `font-faces` entry exists for the requested script.  
So you can point `glyphs` at any reachable URL – even a local empty folder – and the SDK will fall back to the bundled fonts.

> **Bottom line:** keep a `glyphs` field (e.g. `"glyphs": "file:///android_asset/glyphs/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"`), but you do **not** need to generate or host any PBFs.

---

## 2. Bundle the fonts you need

### 2.1 Choose the fonts

| Script / Use-case | Recommended open-source font (free) | Where to get it |
|-------------------|--------------------------------------|-----------------|
| Latin, Greek, Cyrillic | **Noto Sans Regular** | <https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-fonts/tree/main/hinted/ttf/NotoSans> |
| CJK (Chinese, Japanese, Korean) | **Noto Sans CJK SC/TC/JP/KR** (or any system sans) | <https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-cjk> |
| Arabic | **Noto Sans Arabic** | <https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-fonts/tree/main/hinted/ttf/NotoSansArabic> |
| Hebrew | **Noto Sans Hebrew** | <https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-fonts/tree/main/hinted/ttf/NotoSansHebrew> |
| Devanagari, Thai, Khmer, Myanmar, etc. | **Noto Sans Devanagari**, **Noto Sans Thai**, … | same repo as above |

> **Tip:** If you only need a subset of scripts, you can ship just those font files – the rest will be ignored.

### 2.2 Put them in the APK

```
app/
 └─ src/
     └─ main/
         └─ assets/
             └─ fonts/
                 ├─ NotoSans-Regular.ttf
                 ├─ NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf
                 ├─ NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf
                 └─ NotoSansCJKsc-Regular.otf   (or .ttf)
```

*Android assets are read with the `asset://` or `file:///android_asset/` URL schemes.*

---

## 3. Add a `font-faces` block to the style

### 3.1 Minimal JSON (offline style file)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "file:///android_asset/glyphs/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",   // dummy, never used
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [ … ],

  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "asset://fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F", "U+1E00-1EFF"]   // Latin + basic Latin-1
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Arabic": [
      {
        "url": "asset://fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF", "U+0750-077F", "U+08A0-08FF"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari": [
      {
        "url": "asset://fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans CJK SC": [
      {
        "url": "asset://fonts/NotoSansCJKsc-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": [
          "U+3000-30FF", "U+3400-4DBF", "U+4E00-9FFF",
          "U+F900-FAFF", "U+FF00-FFEF"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

*All `unicode-range` arrays are optional – you can omit them if you want the whole font file to be used for every code-point. Adding them just makes the intent explicit and can reduce the amount of font data the native HarfBuzz engine needs to load.*

### 3.2 Using `style.setFontFaces()` at runtime (optional)

If you prefer to keep the JSON clean and inject the mapping from code:

```java
MapLibre.getInstance(context); // initialise the SDK

mapView.getMapAsync(map -> {
    map.setStyle(
        new Style.Builder().fromUri("asset://style/offline-style.json"),
        style -> {
            // Build the same map that appears in the JSON above
            Map<String, List<FontFace>> fontFaces = new HashMap<>();

            fontFaces.put("Noto Sans Regular", Collections.singletonList(
                new FontFace("asset://fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
                            Collections.singletonList("U+0000-00FF"))
            ));

            fontFaces.put("Noto Sans Arabic", Collections.singletonList(
                new FontFace("asset://fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
                            Collections.singletonList("U+0600-06FF"))
            ));

            // …add the other fonts the same way…

            style.setFontFaces(fontFaces);
        });
});
```

> **Note:** `FontFace` is the native class that mirrors the JSON structure (`url` + optional `unicode-range`). The SDK will load the files lazily the first time a glyph from that range is needed.

---

## 4. Make sure your `text-font` values match the keys in `font-faces`

A typical symbol layer:

```json
{
  "id": "place-labels",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "osm",
  "source-layer": "place",
  "layout": {
    "text-field": ["get", "name"],
    "text-font": [
      "Noto Sans Regular",
      "Noto Sans Arabic",
      "Noto Sans Devanagari",
      "Noto Sans CJK SC"
    ],
    "text-size": 14
  },
  "paint": {
    "text-color": "#222"
  }
}
```

*The order is a fallback list – the first font that contains the needed glyph(s) wins. Because we declared each script-specific font in `font-faces`, the engine will pick the right one automatically.*

---

## 5. CJK handling on Native

*MapLibre Native does **not** have the `localIdeographFontFamily` option that GL JS provides.*  
Instead, it **always uses the font file you declare** (via `font-faces`) for CJK characters.  
Therefore:

* **Do not generate or serve CJK glyph-PBFs** – they would be huge and unnecessary.  
* **Bundle a CJK font** (e.g. `NotoSansCJKsc-Regular.otf`) and reference it as shown above.  
* The native HarfBuzz-based text shaper will render the ideographs directly from that file, offline, with no extra network traffic.

---

## 6. Right-to-left (RTL) scripts (Arabic, Hebrew)

*Since MapLibre Native 11.13.0* (and GL JS ≥ 6.9.0) **RTL shaping is built-in** – you do **not** need the old `setRTLTextPlugin`.  
Just:

1. Include the appropriate font in `font-faces`.  
2. Use the script-specific name in `text-font`.  
3. The engine will automatically layout the text right-to-left and apply Arabic joining forms.

---

## 7. Full offline-first checklist

| ✅ Item | What to do |
|--------|------------|
| **Style JSON** | Store it in `assets/style/offline-style.json`. Include a dummy `glyphs` URL and a complete `font-faces` block. |
| **Font files** | Place every `.ttf/.otf/.otc` you need under `assets/fonts/`. |
| **`glyphs` folder** (optional) | Create an empty folder `assets/glyphs/` so the dummy URL resolves (no files needed). |
| **Android Manifest** | Ensure `android:allowBackup="false"` if you want the assets to stay read-only; no special permissions needed for assets. |
| **Map initialization** | Call `MapLibre.getInstance(context)` **once** (e.g. in `Application.onCreate`). |
| **Map style loading** | Use `Style.Builder().fromUri("asset://style/offline-style.json")`. |
| **Font-faces injection** | Either embed the block in the JSON (simpler) **or** call `style.setFontFaces()` after the style loads. |
| **Test on device** | Turn off Wi-Fi/Cellular, launch the app, verify that all labels (Latin, CJK, Arabic, Devanagari, etc.) appear. |
| **Debug** | If a label falls back to a generic system font, check: <br>• The `text-font` name matches a key in `font-faces` exactly (case-sensitive). <br>• The `unicode-range` covers the code-point (or omit it). <br>• The asset URL is reachable (`Logcat` will show “Failed to load font …”). |
| **Performance tip** | Keep the font files as **OTF** (single file) rather than a collection of many small TTFs – the native loader reads the whole file once and caches it. |

---

